WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.905
Someone drops all their books in the school corridor. Do you stop to help? A bystander is a person who sees something happening, but is not directly involved. Bystander intervention means that a witness chooses to help. Helping like this is pro-social behaviour, which means positive behaviour that benefits others.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:42.652
Sometimes people fail to help because others are present. That is the bystander effect. Latané and Darley, in nineteen sixty-eight, explained it as diffusion of responsibility. The more people around, the less responsible each person feels, because they assume someone else will act.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:56.443
Factors fall into two kinds. A situational factor is about the situation around the person. A personal factor is about the person themselves. Always check which kind the question asks for.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:19.846
Cost of helping means people help less when it seems dangerous, embarrassing or time-consuming. Ambiguity means it is unclear if it is an emergency, so people wait to see what others do. Competence means having the skill to help. A question gives a scene and asks for one factor, linked to it.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:37.893
Step one: check whether the question asks for a personal or a situational factor. Step two: name the factor and say in one sentence how it affects helping. Step three: point to a detail in the scene that shows the factor at work.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:44.985
The traps are giving the wrong kind of factor, and naming a factor without explaining how it works.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:02:01.741
Here is a real exam question. Keira sees a younger boy being hurt at school, and walks past. We explain one situational factor that could account for this. Two marks.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:11.928
Step one: the detail. Lots of other students saw it too, and nobody did anything. That points to diffusion of responsibility.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:27.057
Step two: explain the factor, then link it. When many people see an incident, each person feels less personally responsible. Keira may have assumed that one of the other students, or a teacher, would deal with it.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:37.503
A tempting wrong route is to say Keira is shy. That is a personal factor, and the scene never says it. The question asks for a situational one.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.564
The exam answer.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:49.296
One mark for explaining the factor and one for linking it to the pupils watching Keira. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:03:03.683
A common mistake is to name a factor without explaining how it works. Just writing, diffusion of responsibility, is only a label. Say what it does to the person.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:22.018
Now you try. Kareem sees an older woman fall down the stairs of a bus. Explain one personal factor that could affect whether Kareem helps. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:42.974
A personal factor is competence. If Kareem has first aid skills, he feels able to help, so he is more likely to. If he is a student with no training, he may be unsure what to do, so he might not help. Either way, we link the factor to Kareem.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:54.561
A common wrong answer is, lots of people were around. That is a situational factor, about the place. The question asks for a personal factor, so it earns nothing.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.905
So check the kind of factor, explain how it works, and link it to the scene.
